      "full_text": "o holds on to the company\u2019s pursestrings, Robert Khoo says whether it\u2019s all possible. So far, Penny Arcade has been successful with the launch of PAX, a well-attended game conference that now has a handful of events around the world. It also has a merchandising business, which is not as widely known, but no substantial. The company\u2019s latest effort includes filming a reality TV show called Strip Search ,\u201d about finding America\u2019s next top comic artist. The funding for the show, in Penny Arcade: Expanding Beyond Comics Mike Krahulik and Jerry Holkins of Penny Arcade are venturing into new territory with \"Strip Search,\" a reality TV series aimed at discovering America's next top comic artist. This foray marks their expansion beyond comics, leveraging nearly 15 years in the business that began as a platform for video game humor. Historical Context: Penny Arcade started in digital media but expanded into events like PAX and merchandising lines through strategic ventures outside gaming content after breaking free from Cyanide & Happiness. This move diversifies their portfolio, reflecting both creative growth and adaptability to evolving digital storytelling mediums. Transitioning to Reality TV: With \"Bound,\" a well-received movie directed by LaToya Devezin in 2019/2020 (to be confirmed), Penny Arcade has already forayed into film. Now, producing their first reality series (\"Strip Search\") signals diversification towards longer-form content, potentially capturing audiences on platforms like",
