      "preview_head": "Part 13: INLAND FISHERIES AND WILDLIFE HEADING: PL 2003, C. 414, PT. A, \u00a72 (NEW); PT. D, \u00a77 (AFF); C. 614, \u00a79 (AFF) Subpart 1: GENERAL DEFINITIONS HEADING: PL 2003, C. 414, PT. A, \u00a72 (NEW); PT. D, \u00a77 ",
      "preview_tail": "orders except humans and domestic animals (such livestock), including any dead specimen of wildlife. It encompasses all elements usually associated with \u2018wild\u2019 rather than urban, agricultural settings",
      "full_text": "Part 13: INLAND FISHERIES AND WILDLIFE HEADING: PL 2003, C. 414, PT. A, \u00a72 (NEW); PT. D, \u00a77 (AFF); C. 614, \u00a79 (AFF) Subpart 1: GENERAL DEFINITIONS HEADING: PL 2003, C. 414, PT. A, \u00a72 (NEW); PT. D, \u00a77 (AFF); C. 614, \u00a79 (AFF) Chapter 901: DEFINITIONS HEADING: PL 2003, C. 414, PT. A, \u00a72 (NEW); PT. D, \u00a77 (AFF); C. 614, \u00a79 (AFF) \u00a710051 \u00a710001. Definitions As used in this Part, unless the context otherwise indicates, the following terms have the following memeanings: - \"Commissioner\": The Commissioner of Inland Fisheries and Wildlife. - \u201cDepartment\u201d: Unless a different meaning is clearly required by law or usage elsewhere herein, refers to the Department of Inland Fisheries and Wildlife. - \u201dInvasive species: Any species that enters an ecosystem where it does not evolve naturally, including its seeds, eggs, spores, or other biological material capable of propagating such non-native species. These organisms can cause harm economically, environmentally, or ecologically by establishing themselves in a new environment to the detriment of native habitats. - \"Natural area\u201d: An undisturbed ecological community that has exceptional value for wildlife habitat and biodiversity conservation due to its natural characteristics; also includes areas designated as such within this Part after an evaluation confirming their significance under criteria established through regulation or executive order by the Commissioner, in coordination with relevant stakeholders including scientists from universities. - \u201cNon-game species\u201d: Species of animals which are not legally classified for hunting purposes and typically do not have a set season during which they can be hunted. These often include birds like hawks, owls, many songbirds; mammals such as porcupines or foxes that aren't commonly pursued by hunters. - \u201cWildlife\u201d: All living organisms in the animal kingdom found within Maine\u2019s borders except humans and domestic animals (such livestock), including any dead specimen of wildlife. It encompasses all elements usually associated with \u2018wild\u2019 rather than urban, agricultural settings",
      "avg_prediction": 0.3836,
